\DOC LOCAL_DOUBLE_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_DOUBLE_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances the symbolic machine state across an inlined "double modulo p_25519"
subroutine block, replacing the current state variable by a successor and
adding an assumption characterising the doubled result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} acting as a positional/offset selector for the
block being stepped over; the second is a {string list} naming the output
buffer variable and the input variable of the doubling step, the first of these
names being used for the new result value appearing in the added assumptions.
Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a state
variable, it yields one subgoal with the same {eventually} statement about a
fresh successor state:
{
   A ?- eventually ... t
   ========================================  LOCAL_DOUBLE_TWICE4_TAC n [out; in]
   A' ?- eventually ... u
}
\noindent Here {u} is a fresh successor state and {A'} is {A} restated for {u}:
every assumption mentioning the old state (register/memory reads, the
{bytes_loaded} assumption, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation, flag
assumptions) is discharged and re-added in terms of {u}, with the {MAYCHANGE}
assumption extended by an extra component for the memory region the block
writes and the program-counter assumption advanced; {A'} additionally contains
a new assumption recording the contents of that region as the result value, and
a new arithmetic assumption that the result is congruent to twice the input
modulo {p_25519} (sometimes guarded by a bound hypothesis on the input).
Assumptions not referring to the state (nonoverlapping, numeric and algebraic
side conditions) are retained, though their order in the list may change.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour on goals
of this {eventually} form.

\ENDDOC
